      {
        "id": "s3_dendrites",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Dendrites",
        "marks": 1,
        "why": "Direction is the whole point of the pair: dendrites bring the impulse in and the axon carries it out, so the words afferent and efferent are what the examiner ticks.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Saying dendrites carry the impulse away from the cell body. They carry it towards the cell body.",
          "Writing that dendrites have no Nissl granules. They have them; the axon does not."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Dendrites bring it in (afferent); the axon takes it away (efferent).",
        "margin_note": "Shape, contents, direction. Three short lines.",
        "mark_note": "Dendrites",
        "lines": [
          "1) Dendrites: short, branched processes arising",
          "from the cell body.",
          "They contain Nissl granules and neurofibrils.",
          "They carry the nerve impulse TOWARDS the cell",
          "body, so they are afferent."
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "s4_cell_body",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Cell body",
        "marks": 1,
        "why": "Nissl granules are rough endoplasmic reticulum, so naming them tells the examiner that the cell body is the part which makes the neuron's proteins.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing that Nissl granules are present in the axon. They are absent there.",
          "Giving only one name for the cell body. Cyton, soma and perikaryon are all asked."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Three names for one part: cyton, soma, perikaryon — and Nissl granules make its protein.",
        "margin_note": "All three names, the nucleus, then the contents. Nissl granules must appear.",
        "mark_note": "Cell body",
        "lines": [
          "2) Cell body: also called the cyton, the soma or",
          "the perikaryon.",
          "It has a large spherical nucleus.",
          "Its cytoplasm holds Nissl granules, which are",
          "rough endoplasmic reticulum and make the",
          "neuron's proteins.",
          "It also holds neurofibrils and lipofuscin",
          "granules."
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "s5_axon",
        "kind": "boxed_final",
        "label": "Axon",
        "marks": 1,
        "why": "The axon's parts are all named after it — hillock, axolemma, axoplasm, telodendria — so the list is easy to hold once the order from base to tip is fixed.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Naming the end branches dendrites. The end branches of an axon are telodendria.",
          "Leaving out the neurotransmitter. Acetylcholine is worth a name."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Follow the axon from base to tip: hillock, axolemma and axoplasm, telodendria, synaptic knobs.",
        "margin_note": "Take the axon from its base to its tip in one order. Acetylcholine is worth naming.",
        "mark_note": "Axon",
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "One axon, efferent, ends in synaptic knobs",
            "style": "boxed"
          },
          "3) Axon: a single long, cylindrical process from",
          "the cell body.",
          "Its base is the axon hillock, its membrane is",
          "the axolemma and its cytoplasm the axoplasm.",
          "Nissl granules are absent in it.",
          "Its end branches into telodendria, which end in",
          "synaptic knobs holding neurotransmitters such",
          "as acetylcholine.",
          "The axon carries the impulse away from the cell",
          "body, so it is efferent."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "insider_note": "Four marks means four blocks — parts, dendrites, cell body, axon — and the axon block is the long one, so leave room for it instead of writing everything about the cell body."
